	[==[{"module":"gui","system_module":"gui","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"Build the UI as ONE governed responsive layer, outside-in in the canonical order (ScreenGui -> root container Frame -> layout/constraint objects -> content -> styling -> script), never element-by-element. Every GUI object is made with create_instance and configured with set_property; all scripting (TweenService feedback, runtime-cloned templates, a ViewportSize-keyed UIScale, a central style ModuleScript, reactive data) goes through run_luau; capture/capture_angles + evaluate(category='gui') close the loop. The four moves that separate this from a generic 'AI did it' UI: (1) Scale-not-Offset sizing under one governed root, (2) layout objects own all placement and spacing, (3) a centralized role-based palette + type scale + token system, and (4) TweenService micro-feedback wired as a system that reaches runtime-spawned elements.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Governed responsive Scale layer","what":"The whole UI is reasoned about as ONE responsive layer whose scaling/anchoring is owned in a single place: a transparent full-screen root Frame (BackgroundTransparency=1) under the ScreenGui, with Size/Position in UDim2 Scale and role-matched AnchorPoint, so children inherit the layer's responsive behavior instead of each carrying an absolute pixel position. Optionally a single ViewportSize-keyed UIScale governs the entire subtree.","how":"create_instance a ScreenGui under StarterGui (ResetOnSpawn=false for HUD, ScreenInsets=CoreUISafeInsets, DisplayOrder for modals). create_instance a root Frame child; set_property BackgroundTransparency=1, AnchorPoint={0.5,0.5}, Position=UDim2.fromScale(0.5,0.5), Size in Scale. For the one-scaler substrate, run_luau to insert a UIScale and set UIScale.Scale = 1/math.max(base.X/viewport.X, base.Y/viewport.Y) recomputed on Camera:GetPropertyChangedSignal('ViewportSize'). All later elements are children of this root and use Scale Size/Position with Offset reserved only for UIStroke thickness and small crisp icons."},{"name":"Layout-driven composition","what":"Repeated/structured arrangements (HUD rows, menus, shop grids, inventory cells, settings lists) are produced declaratively by layout objects (UIListLayout / UIGridLayout / UITableLayout), never by hand-positioning each sibling, so spacing stays uniform and the design survives adding/removing items. Inner margins (UIPadding) are distinguished from between-item gutters (layout Padding), with one repeated padding value.","how":"create_instance a UIListLayout (or UIGridLayout) as a child of each container; set_property FillDirection, SortOrder=LayoutOrder, Padding (a UDim gap BETWEEN items), HorizontalAlignment/VerticalAlignment. Give each child a LayoutOrder integer; leave child Position untouched (the layout overrides it). create_instance a separate UIPadding inside each container for the OUTER margin (UIListLayout.Padding does not pad the list's outer bounds). For grids, set CellSize/CellPadding; for tabular rows use UITableLayout."},{"name":"Centralized design system (tokens)","what":"A single source of style truth: ~4-5 role-named colors (base, background-2, outline, text, one reserved accent for CTAs), a 2-3 tier type scale (header/subhead/body size+weight in ONE clean family like Gotham), and one repeated spacing value — defined once and reused everywhere, ideally with meaning-bearing concepts (Health, currency, rarity) locked to fixed colors and monochrome icons recolored via ImageColor3. At full strength a StyleSheet of StyleRules with $-tokens + Themes, or reusable component templates whose style is defined once.","how":"run_luau to create a ModuleScript (Theme/Tokens) in ReplicatedStorage returning a table of role colors + type scale + spacing. Reference those tokens when set_property sets BackgroundColor3/TextColor3/Font/TextSize on each element rather than picking values per element. Primary buttons = filled accent; secondary = muted/gray/text-only. For the exceptional path, create_instance a StyleSheet with StyleRule children using selectors + $tokens + Theme objects, or define one reusable component template cloned per use."},{"name":"TweenService feedback system","what":"Every interactive element acknowledges input through TweenService-driven micro-feedback — a snappy hover (scale-up via a child UIScale and/or UIStroke 0->2-3px + brightness) and a quick press scale-down that settles — and state changes are animated (panels start off-screen+Visible and tween in), not hard-cut. Crucially this is wired as a SYSTEM that auto-applies to runtime-spawned elements (a PlayerGui.DescendantAdded linker, or feedback baked into reusable component templates) and is bound to the cross-platform Activated event.","how":"run_luau: build a feedback module that, on MouseEnter, plays TweenService:Create(childUIScale, TweenInfo.new(0.2, Enum.EasingStyle.Quad, Enum.EasingDirection.Out), {Scale=1.08}):Play(), tweens back on MouseLeave, scales down on press. Drive scale via a child UIScale (not Size) so layout siblings don't fight it; AnchorPoint {0.5,0.5} so it grows from center. Connect a PlayerGui.DescendantAdded listener that attaches the feedback to any new GuiButton, and bind clicks to button.Activated (mouse/touch/gamepad). Animate open/close by pre-setting the frame off-screen and tweening Position; add a Lighting blur on modal open."}],"common_pitfalls":["Parenting content straight to the ScreenGui with no root container Frame: the whole screen can't be moved/scaled as a unit and layout grouping breaks. Always insert one transparent full-screen root Frame (BackgroundTransparency=1) first; forgetting that transparency leaves opaque gray boxes everywhere.","Leaving inserted elements at the engine defaults — pixel Offset Size/Position and AnchorPoint 0,0. This is the #1 'looks fine on PC, squished/overflowing on mobile' tell. Set AnchorPoint by role BEFORE positioning, then use UDim2 Scale; reserve Offset for UIStroke thickness and small crisp icons only.","Hand-positioning siblings instead of letting a layout object own placement. A UIListLayout/UIGridLayout OVERRIDES each child's Position, so manual nudges silently do nothing and adding one item breaks alignment. Set SortOrder=LayoutOrder + per-child LayoutOrder.","Confusing the two spacing systems: UIPadding is the container's INNER margin; layout Padding is the gap BETWEEN items. UIListLayout.Padding does NOT pad the list's outer bounds — you need a separate UIPadding for that.","A constraint (UIAspectRatioConstraint / UISizeConstraint) on a child OVERRIDES the layout's sizing of that child — place constraints at the governing container level, not stamped on every child, or they fight the layout.","Scale-based CellSize or Scale layout Padding inside a ScrollingFrame with AutomaticCanvasSize creates a circular dependency / corrupts the measurement so items vanish or get cut off; use Offset CellSize and Offset padding in scrollable content, and set VerticalScrollBarInset so the last row isn't hidden under the bar.","Tweening Size directly on a layout-driven child (fights the layout) or tweening UIStroke.Thickness on text (re-rasterizes glyphs every frame, flicker + perf hit). Tween a child UIScale instead, and use LineJoinMode=Bevel/Miter on thick text strokes.","Ad-hoc per-element color (rainbow) and a wall of identical bright buttons: without a reserved accent the accent stops meaning 'click here'. Define the palette once centrally and differentiate primary (filled accent) vs secondary (muted) buttons.","Pure black/white tones and hue-only contrast: use off-black (~35,35,35) backgrounds and off-white text, build separation on value+saturation (survives colorblindness), never red/green-only signaling.","Ignoring platform chrome: buttons under the ~58px top bar / notch / bottom joystick+jump button, touch targets under ~44-50px, and ResetOnSpawn left at true (silently wipes runtime HUD state on respawn). Design mobile-first and verify in the Device Emulator via capture.","Hand-toggled Visible flags that blink, and feedback wired only to load-time buttons via mouse-only MouseButton1Click: animate transitions, and wire feedback as a DescendantAdded/component system bound to Activated so runtime-spawned cards behave identically."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"One governed responsive layer that re-proportions with the viewport: a transparent full-screen root Frame under the ScreenGui, every element Scale-sized with a role-matched AnchorPoint, repeated arrangements owned by layout objects with systematic padding, and a central role-based palette — clearing the architecture-level tells (Offset-on-my-monitor, hand-placed siblings, cramped borders, rainbow color) before any decoration.","steps":[{"id":"gui-build-screengui-root-layer","order_index":1,"instruction":"create_instance a ScreenGui under StarterGui named by function (e.g. HUDGui, ShopGui); set_property ZIndexBehavior=Sibling, ResetOnSpawn=false for persistent HUD, and DisplayOrder (multiples of 100) so modals layer above the HUD. Then create_instance ONE root container Frame as its child and set_property BackgroundTransparency=1 (transparent so it's an invisible governing layer), AnchorPoint={0.5,0.5}, Position=UDim2.fromScale(0.5,0.5), Size in Scale. All other UI becomes children of this root so the whole layer is positioned/scaled once and children inherit it.","addresses_criteria":["gui-fullscreen-container-architecture"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Parenting content straight to the ScreenGui (no root Frame) makes the screen impossible to move/scale as a unit and breaks grouping; forgetting BackgroundTransparency=1 leaves an opaque gray box covering the screen. ResetOnSpawn defaults to true and silently re-clones the HUD on every respawn, wiping runtime state."},{"id":"gui-build-scale-anchor-contract","order_index":2,"instruction":"For every element, set_property AnchorPoint FIRST by on-screen role (0.5,0.5 centered panel; 1,1 bottom-right currency; 0,0 top-left label), THEN set Position and Size with UDim2 Scale (e.g. UDim2.fromScale(0.5,0.5) position, Scale Size like {0.4,0},{0.6,0}). Confine Offset to genuinely non-scaling things (UIStroke thickness, small crisp icons). This pairing makes elements grow from the correct origin and hold proportions across phone/tablet/desktop.","addresses_criteria":["gui-scale-not-offset-layout","gui-anchorpoint-intent"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Inserted Roblox UI defaults to Offset and AnchorPoint 0,0 — the #1 mobile-overflow tell. Mismatching AnchorPoint and Position (anchor 0,0 but trying to center) drifts elements off-screen at other resolutions; mixing Offset Position with Scale Size looks fine in Studio but misaligns on phones. Scale alone still distorts on portrait/ultrawide — add the aspect constraint in the good tier."},{"id":"gui-build-layout-objects","order_index":3,"instruction":"For any repeated/structured arrangement (HUD rows, menus, shop grids, inventory cells, settings lists), create_instance a layout object as a child of the container instead of hand-positioning siblings: UIListLayout for rows/columns (set_property FillDirection, SortOrder=LayoutOrder, Padding for the inter-item gap, HorizontalAlignment/VerticalAlignment), UIGridLayout for uniform cells (CellSize, CellPadding, FillDirectionMaxCells), UITableLayout for tabular rows. Give each child a LayoutOrder integer and leave its Position untouched.","addresses_criteria":["gui-layout-driven-composition"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A layout object OVERRIDES each sibling's Position (and influences Size), so manual Position nudges silently do nothing. Leaving SortOrder at the default makes runtime-cloned items appear unpredictably — always SortOrder=LayoutOrder + per-child LayoutOrder. A UISizeConstraint/UIAspectRatioConstraint on a child overrides the layout's sizing of that child."},{"id":"gui-build-systematic-padding","order_index":4,"instruction":"create_instance a UIPadding inside every card/button/list container and set_property PaddingTop/Bottom/Left/Right to ONE repeated value (e.g. a Scale UDim, or UDim.new(0,8)) so content never sits flush to the frame edges — this is the inner margin. Keep the gap BETWEEN items on the layout object's Padding (UIListLayout.Padding / UIGridLayout.CellPadding), distinct from the container margin. Reuse the same padding measurement everywhere so spacing reads as systematic, and use extra whitespace deliberately to emphasize an element.","addresses_criteria":["gui-padding-breathing-room"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Content jammed against borders is the most common 'cramped' note. UIListLayout.Padding pads only BETWEEN items, not the list's outer bounds — you still need a separate container UIPadding. Conflating inner margins with inter-item gutters makes spacing look ad-hoc. Inside a ScrollingFrame, Scale padding corrupts AutomaticCanvasSize — use Offset there."},{"id":"gui-build-role-palette-module","order_index":5,"instruction":"Before styling, run_luau to create a central style source — a ModuleScript (Theme/Tokens) in ReplicatedStorage returning ~4-5 role-named colors (base, background-2, outline, text, ONE reserved accent for CTAs) plus the type scale and one spacing value — OR store the roles in a Configuration object. Then when you set_property BackgroundColor3/TextColor3 on each element, pull from those roles rather than picking colors per element. Make primary buttons filled-accent and secondary buttons muted/gray/text-only so not every button is equally loud.","addresses_criteria":["gui-role-based-palette"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Ad-hoc per-element ('rainbow') color and a wall of identical bright buttons are the loudest amateur signals and cause misclicks; without a reserved accent the accent stops meaning 'click here'. If colors aren't centralized, a re-skin means editing every element by hand."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Above-the-mean polish: container-level constraints that hold shape and clamp extremes, text/content that scales with the layout, content-derived scrolling lists from cloned templates, animated multi-view switching, a disciplined type + semantic-color + depth/material design system, mobile-first safe areas and touch targets, gestalt grouping and progressive disclosure, tactile buttons, and the known corner/stroke render fixes — all from a structure-first workflow.","steps":[{"id":"gui-build-container-constraints","order_index":1,"instruction":"create_instance constraint objects at the CONTAINER level (not stamped on every child): a UIAspectRatioConstraint on each governing panel/card frame (set_property AspectRatio) to lock shape so Scale-sized frames never skew on iPads/ultrawide; a UISizeConstraint (MinSize/MaxSize) to clamp elements so buttons stay tappable on phones and not absurd on 4K; and a UITextSizeConstraint (MinTextSize >= 9, MaxTextSize) to keep auto-scaled text legible. Drive UICorner radius with Scale, not Offset.","addresses_criteria":["gui-aspect-and-size-constraints"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A constraint on an element under a layout OVERRIDES the layout's control of that element's size — place constraints deliberately at the governing frame, not on every child (over-constraining every child is a 'big mess'). Never set MinTextSize below 9. Pair a UIAspectRatioConstraint with AnchorPoint {0.5,0.5} since it can shift apparent position."},{"id":"gui-build-text-content-sizing","order_index":2,"instruction":"Tie text into the responsive system so it doesn't stay one frozen size while the layout scales: set_property TextScaled=true on labels (clamped by the UITextSizeConstraint from the previous step) so text resizes with its container, AND set_property AutomaticSize=Y (or XY) with TextWrapped on boxes holding variable/localized strings so they grow to fit. Remember Size becomes the MINIMUM and the box grows from its AnchorPoint.","addresses_criteria":["gui-text-and-content-driven-sizing"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"'Everything scales except the text' (one frozen TextSize on a scaling layout) is the #1 device-correctness tell. AutomaticSize in the same axis a parent UIListLayout fills acts as a flex basis rather than a hard size; AutomaticSize + Scale Size on the same axis conflict. If you adopt a single ViewportSize-keyed UIScale later, drop TextScaled (fixed TextSize scales cleanly with the UIScale; TextScaled competes)."},{"id":"gui-build-scrolling-template-list","order_index":3,"instruction":"For variable-length lists/grids (inventories, leaderboards, shop rows, settings), create_instance a ScrollingFrame containing a UIListLayout/UIGridLayout, then run_luau to clone ONE reusable item-template Frame per data entry (set its LayoutOrder + content, parent into the layout). Make the scroll extent content-derived: set_property AutomaticCanvasSize=Y (or bind CanvasSize to the layout's AbsoluteContentSize via GetPropertyChangedSignal) so the canvas grows/shrinks with the item set and survives add/remove. set_property VerticalScrollBarInset=ScrollBar so content isn't hidden under the bar.","addresses_criteria":["gui-auto-canvas-scrolling"],"tools":["create_instance","run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Hard-setting CanvasSize breaks the first time the item count changes. Scale-based layout Padding or Scale CellSize inside a ScrollingFrame corrupts the AutomaticCanvasSize measurement (children get excluded) — use Offset padding/CellSize in scrollable content. Forgetting VerticalScrollBarInset hides the last row/column under the scrollbar."},{"id":"gui-build-paged-panel-switching","order_index":4,"instruction":"Switch between panels/screens/tabs (settings categories, shop pages, menu views) through a systematized, ANIMATED mechanism, not hand-toggled Visible flags: create_instance a UIPageLayout in the holder frame with each page a sibling Frame (set_property Animated=true, EasingStyle, EasingDirection, TweenTime, optionally Circular), OR run_luau to drive reusable templated panels whose open/close/switch is one tweened operation. Add an inner UIPadding so pages center without the next page peeking at the edge.","addresses_criteria":["gui-page-layout-multiscreen"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Hand-toggled Visible flags blink in/out (the 'made in 10 minutes' tell). Without inner page UIPadding the adjacent UIPageLayout page peeks at the edge — bump the padding to clip it. A single static screen with no transitions fails this criterion outright."},{"id":"gui-build-typographic-hierarchy","order_index":5,"instruction":"Establish a 2-3 tier header/subhead/body hierarchy through SIZE and WEIGHT within ONE clean non-decorative family (Gotham in-engine): run_luau or set_property FontFace/Font + TextSize from the type scale in your tokens module (bold headers carrying scannable info, lighter body). Keep to 2-3 text colors. In rows, set_property TextXAlignment so labels are Left and values are Right (the clean-row pattern), and de-emphasize secondary text with TextTransparency ~0.1 rather than shrinking it into illegibility.","addresses_criteria":["gui-typographic-hierarchy"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"One undifferentiated text size everywhere, or mixed/decorative/Legacy fonts, is an instant amateur tell and leaves the screen unscannable. Left-stacking every row (no label-left/value-right) reads as haphazard. Shrinking secondary text into illegibility (vs ~0.1 TextTransparency) destroys hierarchy."},{"id":"gui-build-semantic-color-and-mood","order_index":6,"instruction":"Reuse the defined-once palette from the tokens module systematically across the WHOLE UI (a single source of color truth), and lock meaning-bearing concepts (Health, each currency, rarity) to a fixed color repeated on that concept's text + icon + bar everywhere it appears — set_property the same Color3 from the token wherever the concept shows. Make the palette/mood a deliberate, genre-matched choice (playful sim -> bright/high-chroma; serious/competitive -> restrained dark), not an engine-reflex gray/white default.","addresses_criteria":["gui-semantic-color-consistency","gui-theme-to-mood-match"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Default gray/white or per-screen ad-hoc color carries no consistency. A concept whose color drifts between its text, icon, and bar reads as careless. Shipping a generic look regardless of the game (no stated genre->palette reasoning) only reaches partial — state the emotional tone first, then pull the palette to match."},{"id":"gui-build-depth-material-and-shapes","order_index":7,"instruction":"Give every panel/button authored material instead of flat square rectangles: create_instance a UICorner (set CornerRadius with a Scale UDim) PLUS at least one deliberate depth layer — a thin semi-transparent UIStroke (border, dark not white), a soft drop-shadow ImageLabel placed BEHIND the frame, and/or a subtle top-down UIGradient (lighter at top). For focal surfaces, escape the default square primitive with custom transparent-PNG art via an ImageLabel/ImageButton (white-base so ImageColor3/UIGradient recolors per theme/state). Apply the rounded/styled treatment consistently across surfaces.","addresses_criteria":["gui-layered-depth-material","gui-custom-asset-shapes"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Flat single-color frames with square corners are the clearest 'AI did it' surface; white outlines wash out edges (use a darkened version of the fill color). Avoid shadows ON icons and strokes on bare text. A fixed-Offset UICorner radius won't scale with a responsive layout — use Scale. For nested rounded boxes set outer radius = inner radius + padding so corners stay concentric."},{"id":"gui-build-unified-icon-family","order_index":8,"instruction":"Draw repeated small visual elements (icons, indicators, status glyphs, button glyphs) as ONE unified family governed centrally: create_instance ImageLabels/ImageButtons using a single consistent monochrome (white-base) icon style and set_property ImageColor3 from the palette so a hover/selected/disabled state is one tint change. Load icons via the rbxassetid:// string in the Image property. Drive every icon's color from the design system rather than shipping mismatched, multicolor, or default-gray glyphs.","addresses_criteria":["gui-unified-icon-set"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Mismatched/default-gray/ungoverned glyphs read as unfinished. Assigning a bare numeric ID (or an http URL) to Image silently fails to load — use the full 'rbxassetid://NUMBER' string. ImageTransparency (the picture) and BackgroundTransparency (the box) are different properties."},{"id":"gui-build-contrast-readability","order_index":9,"instruction":"Engineer text/background contrast for legibility and accessibility: set_property off-black backgrounds (~Color3.fromRGB(35,35,35)) and off-white text — never pure #000/#FFF — and build element separation on VALUE (lightness) + SATURATION rather than hue alone so it survives colorblindness (~1 in 12 men). Never rely on red/green-only signaling. Verify text/background pairs against a contrast checker.","addresses_criteria":["gui-contrast-readability"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"Pure black-on-white causes eye strain and pure-black panels read cheap; hue-only 'orange on blue' contrast vibrates and collapses for colorblind players. Shrinking secondary text into illegibility (instead of ~0.1 TextTransparency) destroys hierarchy."},{"id":"gui-build-safe-zones-touch-targets","order_index":10,"instruction":"Design the HUD mobile-first: set_property ScreenGui.ScreenInsets=CoreUISafeInsets (DeviceSafeInsets for notch-only) so descendants clear the Roblox top bar and screen cutouts, keep interactive elements clear of the ~58px top bar / chat / leaderboard and the bottom-left joystick + bottom-right jump button, and size touch targets ~44-50px minimum (~0.06 scale). Layer modals via DisplayOrder, keep persistent HUD ResetOnSpawn=false. run_luau GuiService:GetGuiInset() when positioning by code, and capture/capture_angles in the Device Emulator to verify on a notched phone.","addresses_criteria":["gui-safe-zones-touch-targets"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"Ignoring the topbar/notch and mobile control zones puts buttons under the jump button or crops them behind a notch. IgnoreGuiInset pushes UI under the top bar — only for full-bleed backgrounds, never interactive elements. Touch targets under ~44px are untappable on phones."},{"id":"gui-build-grouping-and-disclosure","order_index":11,"instruction":"Impose readable structure on dense screens: cluster related items tightly in their own sub-frames (each with its own layout + tighter Padding) and separate unrelated groups with larger whitespace gutters, so categories read by proximity without labels. Then triage real estate via progressive disclosure — surface only the 3-5 controls relevant to the current context and defer the rest behind the tabs/scrolling/collapsible panels you already built, swapping action sets by game state via run_luau — instead of dumping every control on one static screen.","addresses_criteria":["gui-gestalt-grouping","gui-progressive-disclosure"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"One even grid with uniform gaps across unrelated items makes unrelated things look related (gestalt fail). Dumping every control on a permanent static screen overloads the player. Separating groups by space alone (without shared visual treatment) reads only partly."},{"id":"gui-build-tactile-buttons","order_index":12,"instruction":"Build each button as a physical, pressable object: a colored container (distinct from the background, rounded via UICorner, bold centered text) holding the label/icon, with a soft drop shadow BELOW and a subtle lighter top-edge highlight so it reads as raised — and expand the clickable region to the whole button/row. Reuse platform conventions for muscle memory: an X close button top-right, conventional dismiss. Wire clicks via button.Activated in run_luau.","addresses_criteria":["gui-tactile-button-affordance"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Bare TextButtons / single-flat-color rectangles don't read as clickable, and novelty controls in odd places force relearning. A tiny inner hit target (vs the whole row) is hard to tap on phones."},{"id":"gui-build-corner-render-fixes","order_index":13,"instruction":"Anticipate and solve the known rounded-corner/stroke render artifacts where the relevant elements exist: for thick text strokes set_property UIStroke.LineJoinMode=Bevel (or Miter) so letters don't show through at high thickness; for a rounded progress/health bar, create_instance a CanvasGroup as the bar background (ClipsDescendants is on by default) and parent the fill bar inside it so the rounded fill is clipped and doesn't poke past the UICorner'd background at low values.","addresses_criteria":["gui-corner-render-fixes"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Default UIStroke LineJoinMode=Round causes visible overlap/see-through where letters meet at high thickness. A naked UICorner on a shrinking bar bugs out and doesn't fit the background at small values — the CanvasGroup's clipping is the fix."},{"id":"gui-build-structure-first-workflow","order_index":14,"instruction":"Work structure-first, not element-by-element: before decorating, establish the hierarchy/layout and lock one consistent style up front. create_instance the grayscale region-zoning blockout (objectives top, gameplay center, controls in thumb zones) and/or lock the style on one sample frame, then propagate it across all frames via the layout objects and reusable templates already chosen — so the result is a coherent skeleton rather than elements placed one at a time. (Optionally mock the whole UI in an external editor first to lock one visual language.)","addresses_criteria":["gui-design-first-workflow"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Decorating first and structuring never (ad-hoc absolute placement) is exactly what a beginner ships. Hand-styling each element independently is why pieces clash — lock and propagate one style instead."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Design-system-grade craft: layout-distributed flex space, a single multi-axis focal point, centralized style tokens/StyleSheet, an elevation/shadow system, scale-aware 9-slice surfaces, TweenService feedback wired as a system to runtime-spawned elements with a tone-matched easing personality, RichText inline emphasis, a reusable prop/state component architecture, presentation/data separation, and a header/body/footer composition that steers the eye.","steps":[{"id":"gui-build-flex-space-distribution","order_index":1,"instruction":"Let the layout system distribute leftover space instead of hand-coded pixel gaps: on a UIListLayout set_property HorizontalFlex/VerticalFlex=Fill for equal-width tabs (or SpaceBetween/SpaceAround/SpaceEvenly), and/or create_instance a UIFlexItem on specific children (FlexMode=Grow/Fill/Shrink) to act as a spacer or grow a child — so nav strips fill the width and items space evenly under resize. At minimum, drive every inter-item gap from the layout object's Padding/alignment so spacing reflows on resize.","addresses_criteria":["gui-flex-space-distribution"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Faking even spacing with fixed pixel gaps breaks on resize. Flex + wrapping add per-frame layout cost — don't enable flex on huge dynamic lists you rebuild every frame. AutomaticSize interacts with flex as 'automatic flex basis', changing how sizes resolve."},{"id":"gui-build-single-focal-emphasis","order_index":2,"instruction":"Make ONE element win the eye by stacking MULTIPLE emphasis signals on it at once while muting the rest: pick the single featured element (primary CTA, featured gamepass, top bundle) and set_property to give it larger area AND brighter/more-saturated color (from the accent role) AND extra surrounding whitespace (a larger UIPadding) AND a distinguishing accent stripe/badge (an extra create_instance'd Frame/ImageLabel) — while deliberately de-emphasizing peers' size and color, so the eye lands where you chose.","addresses_criteria":["gui-single-focal-emphasis"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"A flat grid where every card/button has equal size/color/weight gives the eye nowhere to land (the single biggest tell of unintentional design). Emphasizing on only one axis (just bigger, or just a different color) leads weakly — stack 2+ axes."},{"id":"gui-build-style-token-system","order_index":3,"instruction":"Centralize all visual design so a restyle is a single change: create_instance a StyleSheet with StyleRule children using selectors + $-tokens + Theme objects (light/dark), OR run_luau to build a code-side token/component system — reusable component templates whose style is defined once in the tokens module and propagated — so colors/corners/spacing live in one place. Changing the look becomes one edit, not per-instance copy-paste.","addresses_criteria":["gui-styling-tokens-system"],"tools":["create_instance","run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Copy-pasting identical (or default) properties onto every instance makes restyling a manual slog and themes impossible. A partial token table with many properties still per-instance only reaches partial."},{"id":"gui-build-elevation-shadow-system","order_index":4,"instruction":"Make depth a consistent ELEVATION system mapped to layer importance, not a uniformly flat plane: create_instance native UIShadow on surfaces (set_property tuned BlurRadius/Offset/Spread/Transparency) so shadows grow softer/larger for higher layers — small soft on HUD, larger on panels, largest on modals — and/or use per-corner UICorner radii for distinctive shapes. The shadow size communicates which layer an element sits on.","addresses_criteria":["gui-native-shadow-elevation"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A uniformly flat z=0 UI with square corners (or duplicate-frame/9-slice shadow hacks) reads as generic. Uniform corner radius with no shadow/elevation language only reaches partial — map shadow size to importance across HUD/panel/modal tiers."},{"id":"gui-build-nine-slice-surfaces","order_index":5,"instruction":"Make resizable surfaces hold crisp corners and undistorted proportions under resize: for custom-art panels/buttons create_instance an ImageLabel/ImageButton and set_property ScaleType=Slice with a defined SliceCenter (the 3x3 divider) and SliceScale so corners stay crisp while edges stretch on one axis and the center fills (ScaleType=Tile to repeat textures). For Frame-based surfaces, size by Scale, hold proportion with a UIAspectRatioConstraint, and keep UICorner radius consistent across sizes.","addresses_criteria":["gui-nine-slice-panels"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Stretching a button image warps its corners, and fixed-offset square frames distort/break on resize. Process icons with alpha-bleed to avoid edge halos; avoid baked-in shadows on sliced art that break when scaled."},{"id":"gui-build-tweened-feedback-system","order_index":6,"instruction":"Wire TweenService micro-feedback as a SYSTEM, input-correct and scalable: run_luau a feedback module that, on MouseEnter, plays a snappy ~0.2s hover (scale-up via a CHILD UIScale and/or UIStroke 0->2-3px + brightness shift) and a press scale-down that settles; animate panel open/close by pre-setting the frame off-screen (Visible=true) and tweening Position in (Back ease overshoot), and add a Lighting blur on modal open. Make it auto-apply to runtime-spawned/templated elements via a PlayerGui.DescendantAdded linker (or bake it into reusable component templates) and bind interaction to button.Activated (mouse/touch/gamepad).","addresses_criteria":["gui-tweened-state-feedback","gui-runtime-feedback-wiring"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Tweening Size on a layout-driven child fights the layout (tween a child UIScale instead); tweening UIStroke.Thickness on text re-rasterizes glyphs every frame (flicker + perf). Static buttons and blink-in menus are the clearest 'made in 10 minutes' tell. Feedback only on load-time buttons wired to mouse-only MouseButton1Click misses runtime-spawned cards and non-mouse input."},{"id":"gui-build-easing-personality-stagger","order_index":7,"instruction":"Give the animation a deliberate, consistent PERSONALITY: in run_luau choose one easing family matched to the UI's tone (Sine/Quad Out for calm/premium, Back/Elastic for playful) and apply it consistently across ALL transitions via a shared TweenInfo, and stagger list/grid child reveals (a small incremental task.wait/delay per child) for a cascading entrance.","addresses_criteria":["gui-easing-personality-and-stagger"],"tools":["run_luau"],"pitfalls":"No animation, or one undifferentiated default/linear tween with no tonal intent, fails this. A bare 'it has tweens' without a chosen tone-matched family only reaches partial."},{"id":"gui-build-richtext-emphasis","order_index":8,"instruction":"Carry in-content emphasis through a system, not one flat treatment: set_property RichText=true on labels and embed inline tags in a single string (<b>, <font color=.. size=.. weight=..>, <stroke>, <br/>) to color/weight parts of it — damage numbers, highlighted keywords, label-vs-value — escaping literal <>& and re-applying after localization. At minimum, apply the palette accent to the text that should stand out within your multi-tier weighted type scale.","addresses_criteria":["gui-rich-text-inline"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"One default font size and one flat color on every label means nothing stands out. RichText markup is stripped by localization — re-apply after translating; unescaped < > & break the parse."},{"id":"gui-build-component-and-store","order_index":9,"instruction":"Build stateful UI as reusable components separated from data: run_luau to define ONE component (a function/template taking props) reused with different props (one Menu/Card reused), with visibility/values driven by state and structure declarative — not duplicated hand-placed instances. Then decouple presentation from data: feed the components FROM data (one template instantiated per data item), and at full strength subscribe the UI to an external reactive store (e.g. Charm) with a view-state-vs-game-state boundary, driving continuous/per-frame animation through bindings (useBinding)/springs rather than state.","addresses_criteria":["gui-component-architecture-react","gui-state-store-separation"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Hand-placed duplicated instances per screen can't scale or stay consistent. Baking game data into static instances is the coupling that makes UI unmaintainable — render from data through a reusable template layer; keep UI-only view state (open menu, selected tab) in the component layer and game state (coins, inventory) in the store."},{"id":"gui-build-header-body-footer-flow","order_index":10,"instruction":"Compose complex screens as a deliberate header/body/footer information system that steers the eye: create_instance a vertical composition (a framing header and footer Frame around a distinct body Frame, e.g. darker header/footer framing a lighter body) and add a stated eye-steering mechanism toward key info — color weight and/or UIGradient direction and/or a focal-flow — so the screen reads as a navigable information system rather than a flat undifferentiated grid.","addresses_criteria":["gui-header-body-footer-flow"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A flat undifferentiated hand-placed layout with default colors and no composition fails. Stating focal flow without a framing structure (or vice versa) only reaches partial — commit to both the header/body/footer framing AND the eye-steering mechanism. Close the loop with capture/capture_angles then evaluate(category='gui'), refining the lowest-scoring criteria first."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/user-interface-design/implement-designs-in-studio.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/position-and-size.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/UIListLayout.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/UIGridLayout.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/appearance-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/reference/engine/classes/UIListLayout.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/appearance-modifiers.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/en-us/ui/buttons.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/designing-ui-tips-and-best-practices/3074034","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/user-interface-design/implement-designs-in-studio.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/UIScale","https://monzter.dev/lessons/creating-perfectly-scaling-guis/","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/scale-and-offset-hopes-this-helps/3193639","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/list-flex-layouts.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/ui/list-flex-layouts.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/size-modifiers.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/scale-vs-offset/188291","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/animation.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/on-screen-containers.md","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/production/publishing/accessibility.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/ui/animation","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/scale-and-offset-hopes-this-helps/3193639","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/ui/list-flex-layouts.md","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/UIPageLayout","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-does-my-ui-suck/4019497","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-does-my-ui-suck/4019497","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-does-my-ui-suck/4019497","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-settings-ui/1568740","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-does-my-ui-suck/4019497","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/why-does-my-ui-suck/4019497","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/my-ui-is-bad-dont-know-how-to-fix/2154422","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-can-i-improve-my-ui/2189652","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-settings-ui/1568740","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-my-settings-ui/1568740","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/warning-ui-feels-wrong/1560840","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/ui-looks-very-bad/2614669","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Fg1pxPrzY (Kingkade 3D); corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TWekIGBhx70 (TheDevKing), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7iZWVbq9A1c (BrawlDev), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aSwGwX7HbOE (Gamer M8)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aSwGwX7HbOE (Gamer M8, 788K views)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aSwGwX7HbOE (Gamer M8); same toggle pattern in https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CRFzunf6MWU (Reactive Dev)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TWekIGBhx70 (TheDevKing, verified); corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM (Itz_FloppyFish)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE (Stewiepfing); UICorner basics https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Fg1pxPrzY (Kingkade 3D)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM (Itz_FloppyFish, 177K views, 40-min deep dive); UIAspectRatioConstraint device fix corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE (Stewiepfing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7iZWVbq9A1c (BrawlDev, verified, HUMAN captions — most reliable for exact strings); corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Fg1pxPrzY (Kingkade 3D)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7iZWVbq9A1c (BrawlDev, hover/pressed images); pop effect https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L8Fg1pxPrzY (Kingkade 3D)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CRFzunf6MWU (Reactive Dev, 126K views)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE (Stewiepfing, auto-CanvasSize script); scaling corroborated https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fgBounkghRc & https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM (Itz_FloppyFish)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BLZl-0cl2DM (Itz_FloppyFish, 177K, detailed grid cell scaling); dedicated walkthrough https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fgBounkghRc (Itz_FloppyFish)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE (Stewiepfing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9kuWl_t1AOE (Stewiepfing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TWekIGBhx70 (TheDevKing, verified)"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":35,"criteria_addressed":35,"uncovered":[]}},"date":"2026-06-27"}]==],
	-- locomotion.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"locomotion","system_module":"locomotion","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.1","overview":"Roblox hands every game the stock Animate script — one forward walk/run loop swapped by WalkSpeed — and that IS the generic mean this rubric is built to beat. The whole guide therefore pivots on one decision made FIRST: stop swapping clips and instead OWN a runtime control layer that reads live planar velocity each frame and weight-blends multiple authored clips. You author the clips as flip-book keyposes on a Motor6D rig (spawn_rig + pose_joint/ik_reach + add_keyframe), then write the velocity-driven controller, foot IK, additive aim/lean, momentum tracking, and marker-synced events as runtime Luau via run_luau, verifying every layer visually with capture/capture_animation. Baseline = an owned velocity-classifying controller with a distinct idle@0 node. Good = the killer systems (2D speed+direction blend tree, foot IK, additive look-at) plus stride-lock, directional set, per-region shaping, and velocity-weighted transitions. Exceptional = gait-phase pelvis sway, additive velocity lean, marker-synced FX, runtime momentum, terrain hip-height, a named personality posture, and rig-portable signal-driven computation.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Blend Tree (speed + direction)","what":"A velocity-driven 2D blend space: a speed axis (idle@0 -> walk -> jog -> run) that crossfades adjacent tiers, AND a direction axis (forward/back + strafe, diagonals ideal) whose weights come from velocity projected into the rig's local frame. Replaces the stock hard clip-swap and the moonwalk.","how":"Author the idle/walk/jog/run + forward/back/left/right clips as separate looped Movement-priority clips with add_keyframe (one clip per direction+speed). In a run_luau-written controller script bound under StarterCharacterScripts (named 'Animate' so it overrides the default), Animator:LoadAnimation every clip and Play() them all at weight 0. Each frame on RunService.PreSimulation compute localVel = HRP.CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace(HRP.AssemblyLinearVelocity); derive per-direction weights by clamping localVel.X/localVel.Z against WalkSpeed (X=strafe, Z=fwd/back), and a speed-axis blend factor = speed/WalkSpeed; drive each track with AnimationTrack:AdjustWeight(w) (clamp to a tiny Epsilon, never exactly 0, so tracks keep replicating to other clients) and AdjustSpeed(speed/clipBaseSpeed). All directional tracks MUST be the same Movement priority so the Animator combines them by weight."},{"name":"Foot IK (terrain grounding)","what":"Runtime inverse kinematics that plants each foot on the actual ground, slopes, and steps — layered OVER the playing locomotion clip (the clip carries style, the IK corrects to the world), so no skating and no floating.","how":"Per leg, run_luau to create an IKControl parented to the Humanoid with Type=Enum.IKControlType.Transform, ChainRoot=LeftUpperLeg, EndEffector=LeftFoot, Target=an Attachment you move each frame, Weight=1, SmoothTime~0.2. Each frame while grounded, raycast downward from above/from the animated hip with workspace:Raycast; set Target.WorldCFrame to the hit point rotated to the surface normal via CFrame.lookAt(hit.Position, hit.Position + hit.Normal) so the foot tilts on slopes; reject surfaces past ~45 degrees; disable IK on jump/fall. For anti-skate, FREEZE the stance foot's target at its plant world-position through the contact phase and re-target on lift-off. The IKControl runs in the Animator's IK pass, composing on the clip automatically."},{"name":"Additive Look-At / Aim","what":"An upper-body aim/look-at layer that rides ON TOP of the running gait — head/torso/arms orient to a target while the legs keep walking — never stopping or replacing the locomotion track.","how":"Two routes. (1) Priority route: play the aim clip at Action priority over Movement-priority locomotion so only the conflicting upper-body limbs are overridden. (2) Procedural route (no shipped additive API): in a run_luau script bound to RunService.PreSimulation (AFTER the Animator evaluates), POST-MULTIPLY an aim CFrame onto Waist.Transform and Neck.Transform — Waist.Transform = Waist.Transform * CFrame.Angles(-pitch, yaw, 0) — using math.atan for pitch to the target. Use Motor6D.Transform (NOT C0), keep it to UpperTorso/Head/UpperArms only so legs stay locomotion-driven, and never Stop() the walk track."},{"name":"Pelvis Bias / Hip Sway (gait phase)","what":"The pelvis counter-rotates against the legs and sways side-to-side tied to gait phase (figure-8 hip trace), on R15 via the LowerTorso joint — and the run is re-posed (wider split, deeper drop, an airborne frame, arms in and slightly behind the legs) rather than a sped-up walk.","how":"Re-author the run clip distinctly from the walk with add_keyframe (don't reuse the walk faster). For the sway, in the controller (run_luau) drive a small sinusoidal X/Z offset on LowerTorso.Transform off the clip's normalized time (track.TimePosition / track.Length) so the pelvis bobs in phase with footfalls, plus an acceleration-reactive counter-rotation; apply post-Animator on .Transform onto the cached base so it doesn't accumulate."}],"common_pitfalls":["Leaving the stock Animate untouched and only swapping clip IDs in animTable — that is the generic floor the rubric scores as FAIL on the architecture criteria (loco-state-machine, loco-fadetime-transitions, loco-retarget-rig-portability). An ID-swap is fine to bootstrap, but you must OWN a velocity-driven controller to pass.","Authoring clips on the wrong rig type. Always spawn_rig an R15 rig (R15's LowerTorso/UpperTorso split is required for hip sway and additive aim) and match the runtime rig — an R6 clip does not map onto R15 joints.","Driving locomotion off Humanoid.Running (it fires ONLY on input change, so direction-switches freeze mid-strafe). Poll AssemblyLinearVelocity every frame on RunService.PreSimulation/Heartbeat instead.","Mixing AnimationPriority across the directional blend tracks. All directional/speed blend clips MUST be the same Movement priority so the Animator combines them by weight; a higher-priority track fully wins and kills the blend. Author locomotion at Movement, additive aim at Action.","Letting a blend weight hit exactly 0. Roblox stops replicating a track at weight 0, so other clients see frozen/wrong locomotion (invisible in solo test). Clamp every weight to a tiny Epsilon.","Writing procedural aim/lean/sway on RenderStepped/PreAnimation or via Motor6D.C0 — the Animator overwrites Motor6D.Transform every frame between PreAnimation and PreSimulation, so your offset gets stomped (PreAnimation) or stacks confusingly (C0). Bind AFTER PreSimulation and POST-MULTIPLY onto .Transform (= Transform * offset), onto a CACHED base so it doesn't drift.","Forgetting seamless loops and correct config on authored clips: identical first/last keyframe (copy keyframe 0 to the end), Looping ON, priority Movement (Idle for idle). A non-looping or mismatched-loop clip hitches every cycle.","Mismatching AdjustSpeed's nominal/base-speed constant to the clip's authored cadence — feet still skate even with speed-sync on. Author each clip for one reference WalkSpeed and use AdjustSpeed(liveSpeed / referenceSpeed).","Rig Model scaled below 1 causes persistent foot sliding no animation tweak can fix — verify the rig's scale is 1 before blaming the animation.","Solo-testing only forward motion. The strafe-inversion (right/left swap + negative speed when backpedaling) and the weight=0 replication bug only show under backward/diagonal movement and on OTHER clients — drive all 8 directions and verify with a second client."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"An owned, velocity-classifying control layer drives locomotion (not the stock single-loop swap), with a distinct idle@0 node — the architectural seam every later killer system composes onto.","steps":[{"id":"loco-base-1-spawn-rig","order_index":1,"instruction":"spawn_rig an R15 Motor6D rig AS the substrate for a build-owned, velocity-driven locomotion routing layer — and in the same step commit to that architecture, not just the rig. After spawning, confirm via get_rig that the rig is R15 with a Humanoid + HumanoidRootPart AND that it exposes the LowerTorso/UpperTorso joint split (the seam hip-sway and additive-aim later compose onto) and Motor6D.Transform on the joints (the flip-book authoring channel). Then state the routing commitment the rig exists to serve: locomotion clip selection/weighting will be a function of LIVE planar velocity (and/or Humanoid MoveDirection) feeding MORE THAN ONE locomotion target through a controller the build owns (a velocity-keyed blend space or a custom Animate-override), explicitly NOT a discrete WalkSpeed-keyed swap of one forward clip on the untouched stock Animate. Name the runtime rig type you will author to match (R15 Motor6D) so the authored clips map joints correctly at runtime. Reject the step if it only spawns/confirms the rig without committing to the owned velocity-driven routing layer it is being authored against.","addresses_criteria":["loco-state-machine"],"tools":["spawn_rig","get_rig"],"pitfalls":"Authoring on R6 (or a mismatched runtime rig) means clips won't map joints correctly; R6 also lacks the LowerTorso split, blocking the exceptional hip-sway tier. Always author R15 and match the runtime rig type."},{"id":"loco-base-2-author-idle","order_index":2,"instruction":"Author the idle clip FIRST as a first-class zero-velocity node: pose_joint the rig into a settled standing pose, add_keyframe to snapshot it, then add a subtle breathing/weight-shift keypose and snapshot again, and copy keyframe 0 to the end so the loop is seamless. This clip is the v=0 endpoint of the blend space and the base layer that standing aim/lean ride on. capture_animation to verify it reads as a living idle, not a frozen pose.","addresses_criteria":["loco-living-idle"],"tools":["pose_joint","add_keyframe","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"A frozen/looped neutral default pose with no authored v=0 idle node FAILS loco-living-idle. Idle must be its own node selected at near-zero planar speed, not whatever the stock default leaves. Make the first and last keyframe identical or the idle pops every loop."},{"id":"loco-base-3-author-walk-run","order_index":3,"instruction":"Author at least a walk and a run clip as the >1 locomotion targets the controller will select among. Build each from the four canonical keyposes (Contact -> Down -> Pass -> Up) with pose_joint/ik_reach + add_keyframe: opposite-side (contralateral) arm swing, a small vertical torso bob (two down-beats per cycle for weight), and a held contact pose so the planted foot has real ground-contact frames. Set each to Looping, Movement priority, identical first/last keyframe. The run must be a distinct re-pose, not the walk reused.","addresses_criteria":["loco-state-machine"],"tools":["pose_joint","ik_reach","add_keyframe","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"In-phase arm swing (arm forward with the same-side leg) reads instantly robotic. A flat horizontal torso path makes legs look like they slide. Don't lift the rear foot before the front foot accepts weight, or both feet appear to float."},{"id":"loco-base-4-owned-controller","order_index":4,"instruction":"Write the owned velocity-classifying control layer with run_luau: a LocalScript named EXACTLY 'Animate' in StarterPlayer.StarterCharacterScripts (so it overrides the stock default for every spawned character). Inside, Animator:LoadAnimation the idle/walk/run clips, and each frame on RunService.PreSimulation read planar velocity from HumanoidRootPart.AssemblyLinearVelocity and SELECT/WEIGHT among the >1 targets as a CONTINUOUS function of that live velocity (idle when planar speed ~= 0, walk/run as it rises) — NOT a discrete WalkSpeed clip-swap. This script is the documented hook point all later layers compose onto.","addresses_criteria":["loco-state-machine","loco-living-idle"],"tools":["run_luau","bind_animation"],"pitfalls":"Renaming the script away from 'Animate' or leaving it out of StarterCharacterScripts means the stock default keeps running. Driving selection off discrete WalkSpeed buckets (not continuous live velocity) only earns Partial. Wiring to Humanoid.Running misses ledges/conveyors and freezes mid-strafe — poll velocity every frame."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"The three killer systems (2D speed+direction blend tree, runtime foot IK, additive look-at) plus stride-lock, the directional variant set, per-region shaping, and velocity-weighted transitions — locomotion that responds to direction, speed, terrain, and aim targets.","steps":[{"id":"loco-good-1-directional-clips","order_index":1,"instruction":"Author the directional variant set AND wire it to the velocity-projection direction axis that makes it pass-gate (not just partial). With add_keyframe build forward, back, left-strafe, and right-strafe walk AND run clips (diagonals ideal), each looped at Movement priority. Then commit the selection mechanism: a 2D direction axis whose weights are computed from velocity projected into HumanoidRootPart object space — CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace(velocity) (or dot/cross of velocity vs look/right) normalized by WalkSpeed — so the correct directional target blends in for every movement direction and movement decouples from facing with no moonwalk. Tie each authored clip to its quadrant in that object-space axis (enumerate the forward/back/left/right(/diagonal) → axis-coordinate mapping). capture_animation each clip to confirm a real strafe/backpedal (not a forward clip rotated), and verify a non-forward input weights its own variant rather than falling back to the forward loop. Keep stride length and keyframe spacing symmetric between left and right steps so no leg whips; a missing quadrant must not silently fall back to forward (moonwalk).","addresses_criteria":["loco-directional-variant-set"],"tools":["add_keyframe","pose_joint","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"A missing directional quadrant falls back to the forward walk (moonwalk) or throws animation errors. You need BOTH walk and run variants per direction. Keep stride length and keyframe spacing symmetric between left and right steps or one leg whips."},{"id":"loco-good-2-blend-tree","order_index":2,"instruction":"In the owned controller (run_luau), build the KILLER SYSTEM blend tree: Play() all directional+speed clips at weight 0, then each frame compute localVel = HRP.CFrame:VectorToObjectSpace(HRP.AssemblyLinearVelocity); weight each direction by clamping localVel.X (strafe) and localVel.Z (fwd/back) against WalkSpeed, and crossfade the speed axis (idle@0 -> walk -> run) by speed/WalkSpeed. Drive every track with AnimationTrack:AdjustWeight (Epsilon-clamped, never exactly 0) so adjacent directions/tiers cross-blend by weight. This commits BOTH axes to live velocity.","addresses_criteria":["loco-blend-tree-speed-and-direction","loco-directional-variant-set","loco-state-machine"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_animation","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"All blend tracks MUST share Movement priority or the highest fully wins and kills the blend. Don't hard-switch on a threshold (if Forward>0.5) — that pops; always crossfade weights. Normalize weights or fast diagonals double-up and over-drive the pose. Weight exactly 0 stops replication to other clients."},{"id":"loco-good-3-stride-lock","order_index":3,"instruction":"Eliminate contact-phase foot skating with a velocity-coupled cadence lock whose correctness is measured, not asserted. In the locomotion controller, each frame set AnimationTrack:AdjustSpeed(liveSpeed / clipReferenceSpeed) where liveSpeed is read from the SAME live planar-velocity signal that drives your blend space (not WalkSpeed buckets, not a flat tuned multiplier), and clipReferenceSpeed is the authored ground-translation cadence of that specific clip. Calibrate clipReferenceSpeed EMPIRICALLY per clip: play the clip at AdjustSpeed(1), measure the planted foot's world displacement per cycle, and set the constant so the foot's stance-phase world position holds still at that reference speed — do not guess the constant from the authored WalkSpeed. Clamp the ratio to a sane band (e.g. [0.2, 3.0]) so a teleport/dash/large dt spike cannot blow the clip up, and ensure the clamp is hit-tested, not just present. Then PROVE the lock via capture: run at two distinct speeds and on a slope/varied terrain, and via run_luau sample the stance foot's world position across its contact frames at each speed; the planted foot's per-frame world displacement during stance must be near zero (report the residual slip in studs) — a visual \"looks synced\" is not sufficient. If residual slip remains at the ratio extremes, that is the signal to add the foot-IK world-plant pass (the other accepted anti-skate path) on top rather than over-driving AdjustSpeed.","addresses_criteria":["loco-stride-locked-to-speed"],"tools":["run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"Mismatching the reference-speed constant to the clip's authored cadence leaves skating even with sync on. Don't crank a flat speed multiplier — it must be the proportional ratio. Clamp AdjustSpeed so a teleport/dash doesn't spike the clip absurdly. (A foot-IK world-plant in the next step is the other accepted anti-skate path.)"},{"id":"loco-good-4-foot-ik","order_index":4,"instruction":"Build the KILLER SYSTEM foot IK (run_luau): per leg create an IKControl under the Humanoid (Type=Enum.IKControlType.Transform, ChainRoot=UpperLeg, EndEffector=Foot, Target=an Attachment, Weight=1, SmoothTime~0.2). Each frame while grounded, workspace:Raycast downward from the animated hip; set Target.WorldCFrame to the hit point rotated to the surface normal via CFrame.lookAt(hit.Position, hit.Position+hit.Normal) so feet tilt on slopes/steps; reject surfaces past ~45deg; disable on jump/fall. The IKControl composes over the clip in the Animator's IK pass. Verify on a slope + stairs with capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["loco-foot-ik-terrain","loco-stride-locked-to-speed"],"tools":["run_luau","ik_reach","capture","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"Pole=LowerLeg makes the knee rotate weirdly — use a forward pole attachment or none. Raycast from ABOVE the foot so the ray doesn't start inside geometry. Leaving IK enabled airborne snaps feet to phantom ground. SmoothTime=0 looks robotic. IK alone plants height/angle; horizontal foot-lock through stance phase is what stops slide."},{"id":"loco-good-5-additive-aim","order_index":5,"instruction":"Build the KILLER SYSTEM additive look-at (run_luau): either play an aim clip at Action priority over Movement-priority locomotion (so only upper-body limbs are overridden), OR procedurally POST-MULTIPLY an aim CFrame onto Waist.Transform and Neck.Transform each frame on RunService.PreSimulation (AFTER the Animator runs), using math.atan for pitch to the target — Waist.Transform = Waist.Transform * CFrame.Angles(-pitch, yaw, 0). Restrict to UpperTorso/Head/UpperArms so legs keep walking. Capture a walk-while-tracking shot to confirm the gait never stops.","addresses_criteria":["loco-additive-lookat-aim","loco-per-part-easing"],"tools":["run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"Never Stop() the walk track to aim — that's the generic floor. Use Motor6D.Transform not C0 (the Animator writes Transform). Bind AFTER PreSimulation or the Animator stomps your offset. SET (= Transform * aim), don't overwrite, or you wipe the locomotion pose. Don't aim the lower torso/legs or the walk breaks."},{"id":"loco-good-6-per-region-shaping","order_index":6,"instruction":"Differentiate limb motion per body-region so the gait reads weighted, not mechanical, via EITHER authored per-part easing OR a runtime overlay stack. Authored route: set keyframe easing to Cubic In/Out on arm/head swings (ease-out at end-of-swing) with near-linear legs/torso and moving-hold keys so no joint freezes (add_keyframe over the clips). Runtime route: layer >=2 region-targeted overlays over the clip (the additive upper-body aim from the prior step PLUS an accel/gait-phase torso/pelvis layer) so the upper body is driven independently of the legs.","addresses_criteria":["loco-per-part-easing"],"tools":["add_keyframe","run_luau","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"Raw library clips with one baked Linear curve and no per-region treatment FAIL — all-Linear reads robotic/teleporting. Only ONE runtime overlay (no second region split) earns Partial. Keyframe the FULL limb chain (upper arm + forearm + hand) or an un-authored segment fights the pose."},{"id":"loco-good-7-velocity-weighted-transitions","order_index":7,"instruction":"Make state continuity a continuous live-velocity re-weighting of YOUR blend space rather than the stock fixed-time fade between clip swaps. In the controller, transitions between idle/walk/run happen because the blend weights re-compute every frame from live velocity (adjacent states cross-blend by weight as velocity changes) — no discrete threshold swap. Add a speed-scaled stop/settle and a velocity-sign-flip reversal-through-intermediate for full marks. Verify a pop-free acceleration sweep with capture_animation.","addresses_criteria":["loco-fadetime-transitions","loco-blend-tree-speed-and-direction"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"Relying on the engine's fixed-duration fade between WalkSpeed-keyed clip swaps FAILS — the continuity must come from continuous velocity re-weighting of an owned blend space. Discrete threshold switches with a fade only earn Partial. Hard-cutting (Play with 0 fade) snaps the pose."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Polish layers that read as top-studio craft: gait-phase pelvis sway + re-posed run, additive velocity lean, marker-synced contact FX, runtime momentum, terrain hip-height, a named personality posture, and rig-portable signal-driven computation.","steps":[{"id":"loco-exc-1-pelvis-sway","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the KILLER SYSTEM pelvis bias/hip sway: re-author the run clip distinctly (wider split, deeper hip drop, a true airborne frame, arms in and slightly behind the legs) with add_keyframe — NOT a sped-up walk. Then in the controller (run_luau, post-Animator) drive a small sinusoidal X/Z offset on LowerTorso.Transform off the clip's normalized time (track.TimePosition / track.Length) for a figure-8 hip trace in phase with footfalls, plus an acceleration-reactive counter-rotation against the legs, applied onto the cached base Transform.","addresses_criteria":["loco-pelvis-hip-sway-gait-phase"],"tools":["add_keyframe","run_luau","capture_animation"],"pitfalls":"Rigid hips ('no pelvis sway') or a run that's just the walk played faster FAILS. Pelvis motion baked into one clip with no gait-phase/accel coupling only earns Partial. Multiply onto the CACHED base or the sway accumulates and drifts. Needs the R15 LowerTorso joint."},{"id":"loco-exc-2-velocity-lean","order_index":2,"instruction":"Add the additive velocity/acceleration lean (run_luau, post-Animator): each frame compute velocity's deviation from facing via dot/cross of AssemblyLinearVelocity vs the look vector, map to a capped (~10deg, math.rad(value*10)) tilt on the root/hips, and Lerp toward it with a framerate-independent factor (1 - 0.001^dt, ~0.2 feel) — leaning forward on accel, into turns, back on hard stops. Optionally bank the camera from RightVector:Dot(MoveDirection). Verify with a turn/hard-stop capture.","addresses_criteria":["loco-velocity-lean-additive","loco-personality-cog"],"tools":["run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"No lean reads weightless and FAILS. A lean baked into a clip (not velocity-driven) only earns Partial. A naive Lerp(target, dt) tilts slower at high framerate — use the exponential form. Big tilt (>~30deg) on the root looks drunk and fights foot IK — keep root lean subtle, push sway onto the Waist."},{"id":"loco-exc-3-momentum","order_index":3,"instruction":"Track momentum as a live runtime signal and FEED it into the locomotion output (run_luau): each frame compute acceleration (delta of AssemblyLinearVelocity over dt) or a dt-ramped 0..1 speedRatio over accel/decel times, and drive at least one momentum-reflecting output with it — the accel lean/pelvis bias from the prior steps, the blend axis, and/or the MoveDirection velocity multiplier. For full marks share ONE ramped speedRatio across BOTH the blend axis AND the velocity multiplier through a named TweenService:GetValue easing with tuned friction, so starts/stops/turns read weighted instead of snapping.","addresses_criteria":["loco-accel-decel-momentum"],"tools":["run_luau","capture"],"pitfalls":"An instant WalkSpeed snap / loop swap with no runtime momentum signal FAILS. Tracking acceleration but feeding only a tiny cosmetic lean (no shared blend/velocity ratio) earns Partial. A purely linear ratio with no shaped easing is weaker than a named easing curve."},{"id":"loco-exc-4-hip-height-terrain","order_index":4,"instruction":"Extend the foot-IK pass to vertical terrain grounding (run_luau): the per-foot downward raycast already returns the true slope/step ground height — place each foot there (not the flat clip plane) so the character conforms vertically. For the CEILING, also offset the root/hip vertical position toward the lower foot from the height delta between the two foot-IK targets (clamped), so both feet plant with naturally bent knees on inclines and corner-steps. Verify on a slope + stairs with capture_angles.","addresses_criteria":["loco-hip-height-terrain","loco-foot-ik-terrain"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"No runtime grounding pass (feet on the flat clip plane, floating/skating on slopes) FAILS. 'Feet snap down' with no IK/Transform mechanism, or flat-ground-only, earns Partial. Clamp the root/hip offset or it over-drops on steep corners."},{"id":"loco-exc-5-marker-synced-fx","order_index":5,"instruction":"Anchor contact effects to YOUR velocity-coupled playback layer so they stay synced as playback scales with velocity (run_luau): add named Contact-frame keyframe markers to the walk/run clips and fire footstep SFX/dust from AnimationTrack:GetMarkerReachedSignal — because the clip plays through your owned, velocity-coupled blend layer (AdjustSpeed-scaled), the markers stay locked to real contacts rather than drifting like an external timer. Vary the SFX by surface material for full marks.","addresses_criteria":["loco-marker-synced-fx"],"tools":["add_keyframe","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Timer-based events (not anchored to the timeline) drift under speed scaling and earn Partial. Markers only on walk and not run earn Partial. The stock WalkSpeed clip-swap exposes no synced channel at all and FAILS — this only works because locomotion runs on your owned velocity-coupled layer."},{"id":"loco-exc-6-personality-posture","order_index":6,"instruction":"Give the character a non-default movement signature via an owned runtime posture/COG layer (run_luau) that biases torso-lean and pelvis carriage with movement across ALL speed states (reuse the lean + pelvis-sway layers, applied consistently). For full marks make it a DELIBERATE named personality — a chosen COG bias (forward+wide=aggressive, back=proud, wobble=drunk, rigid-centered=military) plus matching foot-width and stomp/tiptoe weight — and state a tone rationale tying it to the game's feel. Capture across idle/walk/run to confirm a consistent signature.","addresses_criteria":["loco-personality-cog"],"tools":["run_luau","capture_angles"],"pitfalls":"The stock emotionally-neutral default pose with no owned posture layer FAILS. A posture layer inconsistent across speed states, or so slight it barely departs from neutral, earns Partial. An owned posture layer with no authored named-character rationale passes the gate but won't earn full marks."},{"id":"loco-exc-7-rig-portability","order_index":7,"instruction":"Make locomotion a signal-computed driver that is rig-portable by construction (run_luau): confirm the controller computes pose/weights purely from live runtime signals (planar velocity / MoveDirection / joint transforms) — the velocity-keyed blend tree + runtime foot IK already do this — rather than being welded to one stock forward clip. For full marks add a HumanoidRigDescription joint mapping (>=15 joints + T-pose, custom rigs as Custom Humanoids) so one library drives differently-proportioned rigs, and branch the forward/strafe direction sign on R6 vs R15 root orientation so strafe never inverts. Verify on two rigs via spawn_rig + capture.","addresses_criteria":["loco-retarget-rig-portability"],"tools":["run_luau","spawn_rig","capture"],"pitfalls":"A stock Animate WalkSpeed clip-swap welded to one forward clip on the single default rig FAILS. One signal only, or motion still partly tied to a fixed clip, earns Partial. On R6 the HumanoidRootPart faces backward so forward velocity reads negative — branch the sign per rig version or strafe/forward invert."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://create.roblox.com/docs/education/build-it-play-it-island-of-move/looping-animations","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/enums/AnimationPriority","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/animation/play-character-animations","https://github.com/EgoMoose/Custom-Character-Controller/blob/master/characterController/Animate.lua","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-do-i-make-my-custom-run-animation-sync-with-the-walkspeed/2818551","https://github.com/EgoMoose/Custom-Character-Controller/blob/master/characterController/Animate.lua","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/default-r15-player-animations-now-include-strafing-and-backpedaling-beta/1999236","https://github.com/EgoMoose/Custom-Character-Controller/blob/master/characterController/Animate.lua","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/directional-movement-system/2647030","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/procedural-foot-planting-with-ikcontrols/2190661","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/better-runtime-support-for-partial-body-and-additive-animation-layering/4663688","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/making-the-torso-lean-to-the-humanoids-move-direction/991536","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/walk-and-run-animation-feedback/3066069","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-make-animation-consistent-with-walkspeed/2713407","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-a-run-animation/4598246","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/feedback-on-walking-animation/1312736","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/r6-walk-animation-feedback/2047160","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/idle-to-walk-animation-not-transitioning-smoothly/1706668","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/solved-animation-issue-moving-weirdly-when-walking/2653489","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/how-to-reduce-sliding-when-moving/2563257","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CKlQJlNuXPI (Pandastroyer); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CKlQJlNuXPI (Pandastroyer); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CKlQJlNuXPI (Pandastroyer); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CKlQJlNuXPI (Pandastroyer)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CKlQJlNuXPI (Pandastroyer); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sr34MYv35XU (script_ing); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Xi4dQ4FOm6E (Scriptix); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aP_VzBxrPOA (devdump)"],"confidence":"high","coverage":{"criteria_total":16,"criteria_addressed":16,"uncovered":[]}},"date":"2026-06-27"}]==],
	-- vfx.guide.json
	[==[{"module":"vfx","system_module":"vfx","version":"1.0","rubric_version":"1.0","overview":"A great Roblox VFX is never one ParticleEmitter doing everything — it is a coordinated STACK of role-separated layers (a bright core/flash, a soft glow/haze, fast sparks/debris, slow rising smoke, a directed/linear element, a synced light), each owning ONE visual job, each driven by authored NumberSequence/ColorSequence curves over particle life, fired as a triggered one-shot burst with a real lifecycle (Emit -> place -> replicate -> clean up), and finished with a lighting/post/game-feel juice layer. Because the agent has no native particle editor, you author every layer programmatically: create_instance the anchor Part + Attachments + each emitter/Beam/Trail/PointLight, then set_property (or one run_luau block) to assign NumberSequence/ColorSequence, LightEmission/LightInfluence, Drag/Acceleration, ZOffset, and Emit()/Debris lifecycle. Build the scaffold first, then layer roles, then curve and differentiate motion per layer, then choreograph timed phases and post. Verify visually with capture/capture_angles and grade with evaluate against the vfx rubric, then refine. The spine that separates an above-the-mean effect from a recolored default is: MULTIPLE role-separated layers + SHAPED lifetime curves + ADDITIVE self-lit authored textures + BOUNDED, actively-cleaned one-shot lifecycle.","killer_systems":[{"name":"Layered role-separated composition","what":"The effect is a coordinated SET of 3-5 emitters/elements, each owning ONE job (bright core flash + glow/haze + fast sparks/debris + slow rising smoke + a synced light + a directed/linear element), never a single ParticleEmitter trying to be blast, smoke and debris at once.","how":"create_instance an invisible anchor Part (Transparency=1, Anchored, CanCollide=false) and an Attachment in it; then create_instance one ParticleEmitter per role parented to the Attachment, plus a Beam/Trail for the directed element and a PointLight sibling for the synced light. Give each layer its OWN Size/Transparency/Color sequences, Rate, Lifetime, Speed, Drag, Acceleration via set_property. Depth-sort with ZOffset (core in front high ZOffset, smoke behind low/negative). A single-emitter effect FAILS this killer system by construction."},{"name":"Authored lifetime curves","what":"Size, Transparency and Color are driven by eased NumberSequence/ColorSequence curves OVER each particle's life (fade-in at birth, fade-out at death, grow-or-shrink Size, a physically-motivated Color ramp) with per-particle envelope randomness — never left at constant values.","how":"set_property Transparency to a NumberSequence that starts near 1, dips toward 0 mid-life, returns to 1 at death (hides spawn/despawn pops); Size to a grow-then-shrink or grow curve; Color to a ColorSequence ramp (e.g. bright yellow -> orange -> dark red so it encodes cooling). Use the optional 3rd 'envelope' arg on NumberSequenceKeypoint for per-particle variety. First/last keypoint times MUST be exactly 0 and 1. Build these as Luau sequence literals in a run_luau block since structured set_property can't express a multi-keypoint curve easily."},{"name":"Additive self-lit authored texture","what":"Energy/fire/magic layers are self-lit (additive blending + low light influence + brightness) and use a real authored sprite — not the default white square at constant lighting.","how":"set_property LightEmission toward 1 (additive: overlapping particles brighten/bloom), LightInfluence toward 0 (so dark scenes don't mute the color), and raise Brightness. Author a grayscale, soft-edged, transparent-background sprite via paint_texture (or use a crafted asset id) and drive ALL hue through the Color ColorSequence — never bake color into the texture. Design the texture on black because additive ignores black pixels."},{"name":"Per-layer differentiated motion","what":"Each role-separated layer moves on its OWN motion profile — fast outward sparks that shoot then decelerate, slow buoyant rising smoke, a stationary core flash — rather than every particle drifting on one shared profile.","how":"Per emitter, set_property a distinct Speed (NumberRange), Acceleration (Vector3: negative Y for gravity sparks, positive Y for rising smoke), and Drag (high Drag ~10 makes a fast burst flare then decelerate; ~0 lets sparks fly). Sparks: high Speed + wide SpreadAngle + high Drag. Smoke: low Speed + upward Acceleration + long Lifetime. Core: Speed ~0.001 (NOT 0, which hides velocity-oriented particles) so it shimmers in place."},{"name":"Triggered one-shot lifecycle with post/juice","what":"The effect is a discrete triggerable one-shot fired on a precise event as an Emit()/clone burst at a placed position, choreographed as timed phases (flash, shockwave, debris, smoke, embers) with a synced one-frame PointLight flash + camera shake + screen flash, then actively torn down (Debris/disable/Destroy) — not a constant-Rate emitter left running.","how":"Set each emitter Enabled=false (idle). In a run_luau script: clone or place the rig at the raycast hit / Attachment, task.delay the phases (Emit core+flash at t=0, shockwave Beam + sparks at t=0.02, enable smoke ~0.3s then disable, embers linger), tween a sibling PointLight Brightness from a spike to 0 over ~0.15s, fire a client camera shake + a brief color-matched ColorCorrection screen flash, then Debris:AddItem the whole rig with delay >= max Lifetime so in-flight particles finish before teardown. Run impact VFX on the SERVER (or a RemoteEvent fan-out) so all players see it."}],"common_pitfalls":["Single ParticleEmitter trying to be the whole effect — the #1 amateur tell. Blast, smoke, sparks and glow each need their own emitter with their own properties; one emitter can only hold one Size/Color/motion profile.","Flat constant Size/Transparency/Color (a single-keypoint sequence) — particles pop in and out abruptly and read as a recolored default. Always taper Transparency toward 1 at BOTH ends and ramp Color over life.","Baking color into the texture instead of using a grayscale sprite + Color ColorSequence — yields muddy, monochromatic, stock-looking particles and kills runtime color control.","Leaving LightInfluence high / LightEmission low on energy effects — dark scene lighting mutes the color and there's no glow. Drop LightInfluence to ~0 and push LightEmission toward 1 plus Brightness for energy.","Overdraw: stacking many large, high-Rate, semi-transparent layers (big glow + big smoke at high Rate) tanks GPU fill-rate and looks muddy. Keep big transparent layers low-Rate; reserve high counts for tiny sparks; cap total on-screen particles.","Unbounded emission / no cleanup — a constant-Rate one-shot left Enabled, or a rig never destroyed, leaks instances and particles forever. Idle bursts at Enabled=false, bound Rate/Lifetime/Emit-count, and Debris:AddItem the rig after a delay >= max Lifetime.","Destroying the rig immediately after Emit() — cuts in-flight particles mid-life. Disable the emitter and let Lifetime expire (or Debris with a delay >= max Lifetime) instead of an instant Destroy.","Parenting a ParticleEmitter to a big Part instead of an Attachment — particles scatter randomly inside the part's bounding box. Parent to an Attachment for a precise point origin (and avoid Sphere/Cylinder spawn shapes on an Attachment — only Box/Disc render correctly there).","Beam/Trail with one or missing attachment — renders nothing. Both Attachment0 and Attachment1 must exist and be separated; Trail width IS the inter-attachment distance, and a Trail only renders while its part MOVES.","Firing everything in one frame / no anticipation — a heavy impact reads flat. Choreograph discrete TIMED phases on their own task.delay offsets (flash -> shockwave -> debris -> smoke -> embers) with a synced one-frame light flash.","Running impact VFX in a LocalScript — only the local player sees it. Cosmetic muzzle/cast can be client-side for responsiveness, but shared impacts must run on the SERVER or via a RemoteEvent fan-out.","Tuning only in the edit viewport — runtime lighting, camera and timing differ. Verify with capture/capture_angles (and a playtest), then iterate Rate/Size/Speed/Transparency in small passes against the contrast/impact principle."],"tiers":[{"tier":"baseline","goal":"A composited, curve-driven, self-lit, bounded one-shot — clears the four baseline killer systems (layered composition, lifetime curves, additive+texture, performance budget).","steps":[{"id":"vfx-scaffold-anchor-attachment","order_index":1,"instruction":"Build the spatial scaffold as the explicit mount for a ROLE-SEPARATED multi-emitter stack, not a single generic emission point. First create_instance an anchor Part (className='Part') with Transparency=1, Anchored=true, CanCollide=false, parented under a named workspace effects folder. Then, BEFORE authoring any look, name the >=3 cooperating layers this rig will host (e.g. core flash + glow/haze + fast sparks/debris + slow rising smoke + synced light) and provision the spatial origins each needs: create_instance a primary Attachment as the shared point origin, AND a dedicated Attachment per layer that needs a distinct spatial role — at minimum a SECOND Attachment offset from the first to define the axis/direction for the directional/linear layer (sparks/debris/shockwave edge), so the directed role emits along a real vector rather than omnidirectionally from the same point as the diffuse body. State which emitter role will parent to which Attachment. This makes the scaffold encode per-layer spatial structure (point origins for diffuse layers, a directional axis for the linear layer, a near-static origin for the core) that a single bounding-box-parented emitter cannot, and makes the whole stack track/rotate as one unit with whatever it is later attached to. Pitfalls: parenting any emitter directly to the large Part scatters its particles randomly through the bounding box — every emitter must parent to an Attachment for a precise origin; a single shared Attachment for all layers collapses the directional role into the same omnidirectional point as the diffuse body (give the directed layer its own offset Attachment/axis); forgetting Transparency=1 leaves a grey box occluding the effect; forgetting Anchored=true + CanCollide=false makes the anchor fall/collide when later spawned on a character.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Parenting a ParticleEmitter directly to a large Part scatters particles randomly inside its bounding box; parent emitters to the Attachment for a point origin. Forgetting Transparency=1 leaves a grey box occluding the effect; forgetting Anchored+CanCollide=false makes the anchor fall/collide when later spawned on a character."},{"id":"vfx-role-layers-core-glow-sparks-smoke","order_index":2,"instruction":"Author the effect as 3-5 SEPARATE ParticleEmitters parented to the Attachment, each owning exactly ONE role, and prove the separation with role-DISCRIMINATING per-layer property values (not just three copies of one emitter): (1) CORE — small Size, high LightEmission (~1, additive) with LightInfluence<1, short Lifetime, moderate Rate; (2) GLOW/HAZE — much larger Size, high Transparency, LightEmission~1, soft round texture, LOW Rate (it is a big transparent layer — keep Rate low to avoid overdraw); (3) SPARKS/DEBRIS — tiny Size, high Speed, wide SpreadAngle, short Lifetime; (4) SMOKE — large Size, slow Speed, long Lifetime, dark Color, LOW-but-nonzero LightEmission/LightInfluence so it still reads as grounded and never as bright as the core. create_instance each ParticleEmitter, then set_property the role-specific values above so the layers differ in brightness, size, speed AND lifetime — a build that names the roles but leaves their Rate/Lifetime/Size/LightEmission identical earns only partial. PITFALLS: making the glow/smoke layers as bright as the core erases the hot-core-vs-soft-shell contrast that is the whole point — the core must be the brightest, tightest, shortest-lived layer and the surround dimmer/larger/softer. Stacking many large high-Rate transparent layers tanks GPU via overdraw, so the big glow/smoke layers MUST stay low-Rate. A single emitter cannot encode multiple roles and FAILS the layered-composition criterion by construction.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Making the glow/smoke layers as bright as the core erases the hot-core-vs-soft-shell contrast that is the whole point. Stacking many large high-Rate layers tanks GPU via overdraw — keep big transparent layers low-Rate."},{"id":"vfx-authored-texture-grayscale-soft","order_index":3,"instruction":"Give every layer a real authored sprite, never the default white square. Use paint_texture to author a procedural sprite (or set the emitter's Texture to a crafted grayscale, soft-edged, transparent-background asset id). Author the sprite in GRAYSCALE so all hue is driven by the Color property at runtime — one texture serves many tinted effects and avoids the muddy 'baked-in color' stock look.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-additive-emission-and-authored-texture"],"tools":["paint_texture","set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Baking color into the texture removes Color-property control and reads as a generic stock asset. Hard-edged or opaque-background sprites show visible rectangles and harsh seams — blur edges to transparent. (paint_texture tiles on PARTS; for the particle sprite itself use a grayscale soft blob image id.)"},{"id":"vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color","order_index":4,"instruction":"Drive each emitter's Size, Transparency and Color with eased sequences over particle life via a run_luau block (a single set_property can't express multi-keypoint curves cleanly). Transparency = NumberSequence that starts near 1, dips toward 0 mid-life, returns to 1 at death (hides spawn/despawn). Size = grow-then-shrink (impact/smoke) or large-to-small (sparks). Color = ColorSequence ramp encoding the phenomenon (e.g. bright yellow -> orange -> dark red). Add the 3rd 'envelope' arg on keypoints for per-particle variety. First/last keypoint times MUST be exactly 0 and 1.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color"],"tools":["run_luau","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Flat single-keypoint (constant) values are the #1 amateur tell — particles pop in/out and look synthetic. Spend ~80% of tuning time on Size+Transparency curves. Keypoint times that aren't strictly increasing (or don't start at 0 / end at 1) error on construction."},{"id":"vfx-additive-selflit-energy","order_index":5,"instruction":"Make energy/fire/magic layers self-lit. set_property LightEmission toward 1 (additive blending so overlapping particles brighten and bloom), LightInfluence toward 0 (~0.1 so dark scene lighting doesn't mute your authored color), and raise Brightness. This 'fake glow' is what reads as energy; the core layer should carry the highest LightEmission, the smoke layer the lowest.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-additive-emission-and-authored-texture"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"LightEmission=1 makes black/dark texture pixels vanish (additive ignores black) — design sprites on black so only bright parts show. Maxing LightEmission without Brightness (or leaving LightInfluence high) under-delivers the glow; they stack. Many overlapping additive particles blow out to pure white — keep Rate/overlap modest."},{"id":"vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup","order_index":6,"instruction":"Bound the effect and make it clean up. set_property a finite Rate (<=400/s desktop, <=100/s mobile) and a bounded Lifetime NumberRange on every emitter; for one-shots set Enabled=false and trigger a bounded Emit(count) instead of a running stream. Then actively tear down: in a run_luau block disable the emitters and Debris:AddItem the whole rig with a delay >= the max Lifetime so no unbounded emission is left running and no instances leak.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau","destroy_instance"],"pitfalls":"Destroying the rig immediately after Emit() cuts in-flight particles mid-life — disable first and Debris with delay >= max Lifetime. A constant-Rate emitter left Enabled is the classic unbounded leak. An infinite Lifetime makes particles never disappear."}]},{"tier":"good","goal":"Differentiated per-layer motion, spatial/directional structure, animated shapes, a directed/linear element, a core+glow depth stack, and event-bound bursts — clears the 'good' criteria.","steps":[{"id":"vfx-color-graded-ramp-randomized-lifetime","order_index":1,"instruction":"Refine the Color ramp to be physically motivated and de-synchronize particle death. set_property the Color ColorSequence to follow a temperature ramp (hot bright core -> warm mid -> cool/dark end). Make Lifetime a Min,Max NumberRange (e.g. 0.5,1) and set a Rotation range + RotSpeed so particles spin at varied rates — this avoids the synchronized mechanical death where every particle dies on the same frame.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-color-graded-ramp-and-randomized-lifetime"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A single-value Lifetime makes the whole batch die in lockstep (gif-like restart). Identical Rotation on all particles looks mechanical — randomize via a range and the envelope band."},{"id":"vfx-per-layer-differentiated-motion","order_index":2,"instruction":"Give each role-separated layer its OWN motion profile so the composite reads as a real phenomenon. Per emitter set_property a distinct Speed (NumberRange), Acceleration (Vector3 — negative Y for gravity-pulled sparks/debris, positive Y for buoyant rising smoke), and Drag (high ~10 makes a fast burst flare out then decelerate hard like an impact puff; ~0 lets sparks fly). Sparks: high Speed + high Drag. Smoke: low Speed + upward Acceleration + long Lifetime. Core flash: Speed ~0.001 so it shimmers in place rather than flying off.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-motion-acceleration-drag-squash-orientation"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"Speed exactly 0 makes velocity-oriented particles invisible — use 0.001 for a stationary core. Every layer sharing one Speed/Acceleration reads as fake. Short Lifetime with no Speed bump means particles barely travel."},{"id":"vfx-zoffset-depth-directional-emission","order_index":3,"instruction":"Author spatial structure: depth-sort the layers and make at least one layer emit with INTENT. set_property each emitter's ZOffset so layers occupy distinguishable render planes (core in front at a higher ZOffset, smoke behind at a lower/negative ZOffset like -5 to render behind props). Then make at least one layer DIRECTED — set a narrow SpreadAngle and an EmissionDirection (and verify the EmissionDirection NormalId after any reorientation) for a shaped cone, rather than every layer puffing 180,180 omnidirectionally.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-zoffset-depth-and-directional-emission-shape"],"tools":["set_property"],"pitfalls":"Without ZOffset ordering, co-located layers z-fight and flicker. EmissionDirection is relative to the parent part's face — rotating the part redirects the spray unexpectedly. Pushing ZOffset too high pulls particles so close they clip out when the camera nears (keep roughly -5..5)."},{"id":"vfx-flipbook-animated-shape","order_index":4,"instruction":"Give turbulent layers (fire licks, fireballs, rolling smoke, splashes) a shape that CHANGES over each particle's life so it isn't a frozen billboard. Set the layer's Texture to a sprite-sheet, then set_property FlipbookLayout to match the grid (Grid2x2/Grid4x4/Grid8x8), FlipbookFramerate, FlipbookMode (OneShot is cleanest for stylized effects), and FlipbookStartRandom=true so particles don't all play the same frame in lockstep. If no flipbook sheet is available, fake it with a strong evolving Size+Rotation curve so the form visibly morphs.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-flipbook-animated-particles"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A FlipbookLayout that mismatches the sheet (4x4 mode on an 8x8 sheet) renders garbled frames. Without FlipbookStartRandom every particle animates in lockstep and looks mechanically repetitive. Flipbook textures cost more memory — reuse one sheet and drop resolution for mobile."},{"id":"vfx-directed-linear-element-beam-trail","order_index":5,"instruction":"Add at least one DIRECTED/LINEAR element authored as its OWN role, visually distinct from the diffuse body: a streak, radial spark spray, connecting tendril, or expanding shockwave edge. For a connecting/energy line use a Beam between the two Attachments (assign Attachment0/Attachment1, set Texture, TextureMode=Wrap + TextureSpeed to scroll, Width0/Width1 to taper, CurveSize0/CurveSize1 to bend, FaceCamera=true, LightEmission for glow, Segments up if faceted). For a motion streak on a moving part use a Trail (two Attachments — their distance IS the width — Lifetime, Texture, Color ColorSequence, Transparency fading the tail, LightEmission). create_instance the Beam/Trail and set_property its fields.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-beam-craft-taper-glow-scroll-curve"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A Beam or Trail stops rendering entirely if either attachment is missing/removed. A curved Beam looks faceted if Segments is too low. A Trail only renders while its part MOVES, and a too-long Lifetime leaves a stale smear — shorten it for crisp slashes. Beams have no collision — a damaging laser needs a separate hitbox."},{"id":"vfx-core-glow-depth-stack","order_index":6,"instruction":"Build bright heat as a stacked PAIR so it reads as volume: an intense, tight, additive CORE layer sitting inside a softer, larger, dimmer GLOW/HALO surround. create_instance two emitters on the same Attachment; set_property the core to small Size + high LightEmission + high Brightness + short Lifetime, and the glow to larger Size + higher Transparency + lower LightEmission + longer Lifetime, with ZOffset placing the core in front of the glow. Each carries its OWN size/brightness/transparency.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-trail-body-core-stack-taper-shortlife"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Making the glow as bright/opaque as the core collapses the depth read — the contrast is the point. A soft, unfocused core kills impact; keep the inner core small, bright and crisp."},{"id":"vfx-attachment-bound-emit-on-event","order_index":7,"instruction":"Bind emitters to Attachments on the moving source with a deliberate LockedToPart choice and fire them as precise bursts on game events. set_property Enabled=false (idle until needed) and LockedToPart per layer (true so particles ride the source; false so a burst is left in world space). Then in a run_luau script trigger emitter:Emit(count) on the exact event moment (a Touched handler, a RemoteEvent, or an animation marker via GetMarkerReachedSignal) — never hardcoded to a fixed world position or left always-on.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-attachment-bound-emit-on-event"],"tools":["set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"Leaving Enabled=true on a burst emitter makes it spew constantly instead of firing once. Emitting at a guessed point instead of the real event/attachment makes the effect float. Wrong LockedToPart makes a world-space burst drag along with the source (or vice versa)."}]},{"tier":"exceptional","goal":"Burst-then-decay timing with a synced light flash, choreographed impact phases, faked volume from a depth-ordered stack, full lighting/post + game-feel juice, and a triggered networked one-shot lifecycle — clears the exceptional criteria.","steps":[{"id":"vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation","order_index":1,"instruction":"Shape the whole effect as a burst-then-decay with anticipation/impact/secondary beats, not a flat even fade. In a run_luau script: optionally play a tiny anticipation beat, then Emit the core/sparks with very short Lifetime (e.g. 0.2,0.6) + high Speed (20,40) + high Drag (~10) so the burst flares then decelerates, then let the Transparency curves decay the survivors. Sync a one-frame PointLight flash to the burst (tween Brightness from a spike to 0 over ~0.1s). Add a secondary beat (a smaller follow-up Emit) a few frames later.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"A constant-Rate stream has no punch — use Emit() bursts. Snapping the light on/off instead of tweening the spike->0 reads as a blink, not a flash. No anticipation/secondary beat makes a heavy hit feel weak."},{"id":"vfx-impact-phase-choreography","order_index":2,"instruction":"Choreograph a heavy impact as discrete TIMED phases on their own delays rather than firing everything in one frame. In a run_luau script use task.delay offsets: t=0 flash (core Emit + PointLight spike); t~0.02 expanding shockwave (a ring Beam or a fast wide-SpreadAngle Emit); t~0.04 radial debris/sparks Emit; t~0.1 enable rolling smoke for ~0.3s then disable; t~0.4 lingering embers/dust at low Rate. Each phase is its own role-separated layer triggered on its own beat.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-impact-phase-choreography"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","set_property"],"pitfalls":"Firing all layers on the same frame collapses the choreography into a single puff. Forgetting to disable the smoke phase leaves it streaming. Phase delays that exceed a layer's Lifetime mean the layer is gone before its beat — match Lifetimes to the schedule."},{"id":"vfx-faked-volume-depth-stack","order_index":3,"instruction":"Fake 3D volume on the 2D-billboard engine by building the effect from MULTIPLE co-located layers occupying naturally different depth planes that read foreground-to-background: a tight bright core/flash nearest (highest ZOffset), a softer glow/haze around it, then diffuse smoke/dust furthest (lowest/negative ZOffset). set_property each layer's ZOffset to order the stack and give each its own Size/Transparency so the parallax between planes sells depth as the camera moves.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-beam-stacking-parallax-volume"],"tools":["set_property","create_instance"],"pitfalls":"Layers at the same ZOffset z-fight and flicker instead of reading as depth. Equal Size/brightness across planes flattens the parallax — the front must be tighter/brighter, the back larger/dimmer. Overdraw from many big stacked planes still applies — keep the back planes low-Rate."},{"id":"vfx-lighting-post-and-game-feel-juice","order_index":4,"instruction":"Finish with the lighting/post + game-feel layer the textures can't provide alone. Add a color-matched PointLight as a SIBLING of the emitter (create_instance, tween its Brightness on the burst). Add Bloom on the bright emissive pixels and a ColorCorrectionEffect grade under Lighting (create_instance + set_property). On impact, run client-side juice: a time/spring-based camera shake (bind to RenderStepped or use a CameraShaker module, client only), a brief color-matched ColorCorrection screen flash, and an optional short hitstop freeze. Drive these with TweenService so they grow/peak/dissipate on one coordinated timeline.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-lighting-post-and-game-feel-juice"],"tools":["create_instance","set_property","run_luau"],"pitfalls":"A fixed per-frame CFrame camera offset scales with framerate — use a time/spring-based shake bound to RenderStepped. Camera shake and screen flash are per-client — do them on the client, not the server. A ColorCorrection flash with wrong saturation/contrast washes the scene; tune those with the tint. Snapping (not tweening) the light/post makes the layers start/stop on different frames and look disjointed."},{"id":"vfx-event-synced-raycast-placed-networked","order_index":5,"instruction":"Author the whole effect as a discrete, triggerable one-shot with a real lifecycle — fired, placed, replicated, cleaned up. In a run_luau script: stage the rig in ReplicatedStorage; on a precise event, raycast to the real hit position, clone the rig and set its CFrame to that placed position (or to the source Attachment), Emit() all burst layers (staggered per the phase schedule), replicate to all clients by running on the SERVER or firing a RemoteEvent fan-out, then actively tear down via Debris:AddItem with a delay >= max Lifetime — never a constant-Rate emitter left running.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-event-synced-raycast-placed-networked","vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup"],"tools":["run_luau","create_instance","destroy_instance"],"pitfalls":"Placing the burst at a guessed point instead of the raycast hit makes it float in the wrong spot. Running impact VFX in a LocalScript means other players never see it — replicate via server/RemoteEvent. Cloning per shot at scale without pooling/culling is expensive — reuse the rig and cull off-screen instances."},{"id":"vfx-verify-and-evaluate-loop","order_index":6,"instruction":"Verify visually and grade before calling it done. Use capture (single framing) or capture_angles (orbit the effect from several sides at the burst peak) to SEE the composite — particle editors lie in the edit viewport, so confirm timing/density/depth as it actually renders. Then call evaluate(category='vfx', subject=<rig path>, design=<your summary of the layers/roles/lifecycle you built>) to grade against the vfx rubric, and refine the fails-first criteria (curves, additive, motion differentiation, cleanup) in small passes.","addresses_criteria":["vfx-layered-multi-emitter-composition","vfx-lifetime-curves-size-transparency-color","vfx-performance-budget-bounded-cleanup","vfx-motion-timing-burst-decay-anticipation"],"tools":["capture","capture_angles","evaluate"],"pitfalls":"Tuning only in the edit viewport produces effects that look wrong at runtime (lighting/camera/timing differ) — capture at the burst peak, since a one-shot is invisible at rest. Chasing exact numbers from a tutorial fails because values are texture- and scale-dependent — iterate by eye against the contrast/impact principle and the evaluate report."}]}],"provenance":{"sources":["https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/particle-emitters.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://devforum.roblox.com/t/introduction-to-vfx-particles/2068650","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/beams.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/trails.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/create-waterfalls.md","https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Roblox/creator-docs/main/content/en-us/effects/particle-emitters.md","https://kitsblox.com/blog/roblox-particle-system-deep-dive","https://create.roblox.com/docs/effects/particle-emitters","https://create.roblox.com/docs/reference/engine/classes/ParticleEmitter","https://create.roblox.com/docs/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/use-particles-for-explosions","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/curriculums/core/building/create-basic-visual-effects.md","https://github.com/Sleitnick/RbxCameraShaker","https://github.com/Roblox/creator-docs/blob/main/content/en-us/tutorials/use-case-tutorials/vfx/custom-part